      "math_academy_id": "e-3564",
      "problem": "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\dfrac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2}\n=\\dfrac{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1} \\sqrt{x+8}}{\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow 1}(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\nApplying the root rule in the numerator, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt{x+8} & =\\sqrt{{lim}_{x\\to 1}(x+8)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the denominator, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}(x-2) & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x+8}}{x-2} & =\\frac{3}{(-1)} \\\\  & =-3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
